\title{Only the C API path writes the \code{\verb~|index~} data layout entry}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-13}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{compiler}
\tag{platforms}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-13}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\p{Four places stamp a module data layout through \code{\verb~|reussir::translateDataLayout~}. Only the C API entry point appends an entry for the MLIR \code{\verb~|index~} type at the pointer width; the attach-native-target pass, the bridge and the type converter do not, so \code{\verb~|index~} stays 64 bits wide there. On a 64-bit target nothing differs. On a 32-bit target the pointer spec says \code{\verb~|index = 32~} while \code{\verb~|index~} is 64. Found while reviewing \pr{986}; it predates that change and was left alone.}
\p{\strong{What would close this.} Move the \code{\verb~|index~} entry into \code{\verb~|reussir::translateDataLayout~} and test a 32-bit layout through each of the four paths.}
